@fields
{
Traditions :: Divine, Occult
}


@handout
{
You dreamt of a riverbank. Wildflowers, unfamiliar yet beautiful, cradled your body. The warmth of [[Godhome|twin suns]] kissed your face, and a soft breeze carried the scent of earth and water. Across the river, a grand city rose—its architecture strange, unplaceable.

Someone lay beside you in the flowers. They were breathtaking, yet their features slipped from your mind before they could settle, like mist in the morning light. They sang.

The song held you, bound by its melody. Minutes, hours—it didn’t matter. The tune was haunting, filled with loneliness, longing. Even as the dream faded, pulling you back to waking, the song remained.

It had carved itself into your soul.

Now awake, it lingers at the edge of perception. You cannot recall the notes, yet the melody is unmistakable—a funeral dirge, or a call to action.

Months pass. Others have heard it too. They surface in radio broadcasts, in screamsheet editorials, in whispered conversations. Some call it revelation. Others claim it’s a trap. Some fear it is not of this world at all.

They call it the Voidsong.

The world is divided. The faithful and the fearful, the seekers and the skeptics. You know only one thing: it is real.

And it wants something.

Whatever life you led before, the Voidsong has marked you. The need for answers burns within you. Your search leads you to [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]], to [[Org/Iconoclasm/index|Iconoclasm]].
}

Johnny tried to make the [[Phenomena/Sentience Distributor|sentience distributor]] carry the Voidsong and found the song itself is only in the listener's head; there is no song out in the world to touch. The distributor is built around carrying a harmony, so the Party concluded they were missing some piece of hardware or a magical connection that would turn the song in their heads into a signal the device could spread.

At the [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]] pilgrimage, when [[People/Johnny|Johnny]] resonated in the crowd, some forty of the guests answered, which told the Party that many of the pilgrims had heard the song themselves.

A Belvedere archival unit came into a new artifact touched by the Voidsong. It was being analysed when someone broke into the facility loudly, passed its defences, and carried it off. The artifact matches what the Party described to Belvedere: a green rock, twelve-sided, floating in a golden case, carrying a harmonic resonance like the song. Anzu's reading of keystone 14 as a sealed compartment that only receives is close enough that Belvedere believes the two are related.

[[People/Anzu|Anzu]] spent part of the downtime trying to put the song into a form he could look at. The papers and corkboard taken from [[Org/Roundhat Gang/People/Tywelwyn Leatherhide|Tywelwyn]] showed that Tywelwyn had been mapping the harmonies of the [[Org/Embercall Industries/index|Embercall]], and had reached the same conclusion Anzu did: neither the Voidsong nor the Embercall can be written out as sheet music. Tywelwyn's answer was to write a set of pieces that were each a constituent part of what he heard, in the way an abstract painting can stand in for a thing that cannot be looked at directly. Anzu did the same, and after several tries produced a sheet of music that hung a little out of the world: translucent, strangely heavy, and pulling away. He worked out that the song lives only in the heads of those who hear it, and that gathered into one object it might pull something toward the void.
